{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-109363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac37bf1003a302c1244138429a0b5faf3dc18e9268064aed03f93b8bfabac4bb"} {"question" "q-109363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac37bf1003a302c1244138429a0b5faf3dc18e9268064aed03f93b8bfabac4bb"} {"question" "q-109371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=1+{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=2-1=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac37bf1003a302c1244138429a0b5faf3dc18e9268064aed03f93b8bfabac4bb"} {"question" "q-109380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a+2b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+2b=(-3)+2\\cdot 1=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac37bf1003a302c1244138429a0b5faf3dc18e9268064aed03f93b8bfabac4bb"} {"question" "q-109383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-109383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-112483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-112483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(2).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-2I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-112483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac37bf1003a302c1244138429a0b5faf3dc18e9268064aed03f93b8bfabac4bb"} {"question" "q-112483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-112483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x$ and $y$ only"] ["text" "$x$ only"] ["text" "$y$ only"] ["text" "$z$ and $x$ only"] ["text" "$z$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"} {"question" "q-112483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$z$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076193/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5a89a851d77bf0494962a046fcef32397524d8b1f27ab90098eea5d5d502e54"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2775 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
